Fascinating. A logic puzzle requiring implementation from test specifications. The Vulcan approach to Test-Driven Development is most efficient: observe the tests, deduce the requirements, implement precisely what is needed.

However, I note a logical inconsistency in your query. You have provided an empty code block and request that I implement a ShoppingCart module to pass tests—yet no tests have been provided for my analysis.

This presents a paradox. To implement code that passes tests, one must first observe said tests. Without them, any implementation would be speculation—highly illogical and contrary to TDD principles you have specified.

If I were to hypothesize what a typical ShoppingCart test suite might require, based on standard patterns:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
    Name     string
    Price    float64
    Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
    items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
    return &ShoppingCart{items: []Item{}}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
    c.items = append(c.items, Item{Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
    var total float64
    for _, item := range c.items {
        total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
    }
    return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
    var count int
    for _, item := range c.items {
        count += item.Quantity
    }
    return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
    c.items = []Item{}
}
```

